 <!-- INTEGRATION -->
 <section id="integration">
   <div class="sec-label">KNIRVSERVER INTEGRATION</div>
   <h2>Wiring Into Existing Infrastructure</h2>
   <p class="lead">DVE Pods are designed to reuse 100% of the existing KNIRVSERVER DVE infrastructure. No changes needed to WebSocket handlers, KNIRVAGENT, or the TEE attestation pipeline.</p>

   <div class="grid grid-2">
     <div class="card">
       <h3>New Endpoints (minimal surface)</h3>
       <table class="itbl">
         <thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
         <tbody>
           <tr><td>POST /api/dve/pod/register</td><td>Validate pod attestation, create lightweight DVECreation, return WS URL + chain session</td></tr>
           <tr><td>GET /api/dve/pod/{id}/bundle</td><td>Download pre-configured dvepod.html with WASM inlined and node config seeded</td></tr>
           <tr><td>POST /api/dve/pod/{id}/attest</td><td>Upgrade trust level (browser-wasm → sgx) with new attestation evidence</td></tr>
         </tbody>
       </table>
     </div>
     <div class="card">
       <h3>Reused Endpoints (unchanged)</h3>
       <table class="itbl">
         <thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>Usage by Pod</th></tr></thead>
         <tbody>
           <tr><td>WS /ws/dve/{nodeId}/agent</td><td>Primary terminal bridge — pod docks here for full KNIRVAGENT</td></tr>
           <tr><td>WS /ws/dve/{id}/inner/{sid}</td><td>Inner tool sessions spawned from docked pod</td></tr>
           <tr><td>GET /api/dve/{id}/supervisor-agent/status</td><td>Pod polls this to show connection health</td></tr>
           <tr><td>POST /api/dve/{id}/agent/tasks</td><td>Submit background tasks when bridged</td></tr>
         </tbody>
       </table>
     </div>
   </div>

   <div class="callout" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
     <p><strong>Key insight:</strong> When a pod docks to KNIRVSERVER, it presents its attestation and receives a chain session ID. From that point, the existing <code>DVEAgentWSHandler</code> treats it identically to a hardware-provisioned DVE. The pod's trust level is tracked via the <code>TEEType</code> field — <code>browser-wasm</code> for browser pods, <code>wasmer</code> for CLI pods, escalating to <code>sgx</code>/<code>sev-snp</code> if the host is a hardware enclave.</p>
   </div>

   <h3 style="margin-top:2rem;margin-bottom:1rem">Trust Level → Capabilities Matrix</h3>
   <table class="itbl">
     <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>TEE Type</th><th>Trust Level</th><th>Capabilities</th></tr></thead>
     <tbody>
       <tr><td>Solo (offline)</td><td>browser-wasm / wasmer</td><td>L0</td><td>Local shell, BusyBox tools, OPFS storage, self-attestation</td></tr>
       <tr><td>Tethered</td><td>browser-wasm / wasmer</td><td>L1</td><td>+ KNIRVAGENT API, task submission, NRN balance queries</td></tr>
       <tr><td>Bridged (WebSocket)</td><td>browser-wasm / wasmer</td><td>L2</td><td>+ Full terminal, inner sessions, on-chain session binding</td></tr>
       <tr><td>Bridged + HW TEE</td><td>sgx / sev-snp / tdx</td><td>L3</td><td>+ Hardware attestation, oracle access, full DVE capabilities</td></tr>
     </tbody>
   </table>
 </section>

 <!-- BUILD -->
 <section id="build">
   <div class="sec-label">BUILD &amp; DISTRIBUTION</div>
   <h2>Build Guide</h2>
   <p class="lead">Building a DVE Pod produces two artifacts: a WASM binary for CLI use and a self-contained HTML file for browser distribution. Both come from the same Go source.</p>

   <div class="steps">
     <div class="step">
       <div class="step-body">
         <div class="step-title">Install TinyGo</div>
         <div class="step-desc">TinyGo produces compact WASM (~3MB vs Go's ~10MB for this use case) and has excellent WASI support.</div>
         <pre>brew install tinygo   # macOS
 apt install tinygo    # Linux
 # Verify: tinygo version  →  tinygo version 0.32.0+</pre>
       </div>
     </div>
     <div class="step">
       <div class="step-body">
         <div class="step-title">Build the WASM binary</div>
         <pre>cd packages/KNIRVSERVER/dvepod
 tinygo build -target=wasi \
   -opt=2 \
   -no-debug \
   -o dist/dvepod.wasm \
   ./cmd/dvepod/</pre>
       </div>
     </div>
     <div class="step">
       <div class="step-body">
         <div class="step-title">Bundle into self-contained HTML</div>
         <div class="step-desc">The bundler inlines the WASM as a base64 data URL and stamps in node config. Output is a single portable file.</div>
         <pre>go run ./cmd/bundler \
   --wasm dist/dvepod.wasm \
   --template templates/dvepod.html \
   --output dist/dvepod-portable.html

 # Optional: pre-seed a specific node ID and dock URL
 go run ./cmd/bundler \
   --wasm dist/dvepod.wasm \
   --node-id dvepod-a1b2c3d4 \
   --dock-url https://knirvserver.knirv.network \
   --output dist/dvepod-a1b2c3d4.html</pre>
       </div>
     </div>
     <div class="step">
       <div class="step-body">
         <div class="step-title">Run in browser</div>
         <pre>open dist/dvepod-portable.html   # macOS
 # Or serve over HTTP:
 python3 -m http.server 8090 --directory dist/
 # Navigate to http://localhost:8090/dvepod-portable.html</pre>
       </div>
     </div>
     <div class="step">
       <div class="step-body">
         <div class="step-title">Run in CLI WASI runtime</div>
         <pre># Wasmer (recommended)
 wasmer run dist/dvepod.wasm -- --mode solo

 # Wasmtime
 wasmtime dist/dvepod.wasm -- --mode solo

 # WasmEdge (high performance)
 wasmedge dist/dvepod.wasm

 # Docker (via Wasmer container)
 docker run --rm -i ghcr.io/knirv/dvepod:latest</pre>
       </div>
     </div>
     <div class="step">
       <div class="step-body">
         <div class="step-title">Download from KNIRVSERVER (on-demand bundle)</div>
         <div class="step-desc">KNIRVSERVER generates a pre-configured pod bundle with node ID and session pre-seeded.</div>
         <pre>curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
   https://knirvserver.knirv.network/api/dve/pod/dvepod-a1b2/bundle \
   -o my-dvepod.html

 open my-dvepod.html   # Immediately connected to your KNIRVSERVER DVE</pre>
       </div>
     </div>
   </div>

   <div class="callout-warn callout" style="margin-top:2rem">
     <p><strong>Note on stdin in browser:</strong> WASI stdin requires <code>SharedArrayBuffer</code>, which needs <code>Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin</code> and <code>Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp</code> headers. The bundler adds a Service Worker that injects these headers automatically for local file:// use, so the HTML works without a server. When hosted on KNIRVSERVER, these headers are already present.</p>
   </div>

   <h3 style="margin-top:2.5rem;margin-bottom:1rem">File Layout</h3>
 <pre>packages/KNIRVSERVER/dvepod/
 ├── cmd/
 │   ├── dvepod/main.go          <span class="cm"># TinyGo WASM entrypoint</span>
 │   └── bundler/main.go         <span class="cm"># HTML bundler (inlines WASM as base64)</span>
 ├── internal/
 │   ├── agent/                  <span class="cm"># KNIRVAGENT solo + tethered modes</span>
 │   ├── shell/                  <span class="cm"># Command parser, line editor, history</span>
 │   ├── tee/                    <span class="cm"># TEE context, attestation, key management</span>
 │   ├── tools/                  <span class="cm"># BusyBox: ls, cat, curl, grep, ps, …</span>
 │   └── storage/                <span class="cm"># WASI filesystem + encrypted export</span>
 ├── templates/
 │   └── dvepod.html             <span class="cm"># HTML template (JS WASI host, xterm.js)</span>
 ├── dist/                       <span class="cm"># Build output (gitignored)</span>
 └── go.mod                      <span class="cm"># Standalone Go module (TinyGo compatible)</span>

 packages/KNIRVSERVER/backend/internal/web/
 └── dve_pod_handler.go          <span class="cm"># 3 new endpoints, ~200 LOC</span></pre>
 </section>

